  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 AM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nA few patchy areas of shallow ground fog may develop early this \nmorning across central WI with clear skies and calm winds, otherwise \nwith high pressure moving across the region no impactful weather \nis expected today \n\nStorm Chances Return Late Friday: Next chance for \nshowers/thunderstorms comes late Friday night into Saturday morning \nas an upper trough drags a cold front across the region. The risk \nfor any severe storms is very low (less than 5%) as forecast \nsoundings show a stout capping inversion during the period. Guidance \nalso continues to show the axis of greatest instability and \nmoisture remaining well west of the forecast area. There is still \nsome timing uncertainty with when rain may come to an end Saturday\nas recent model guidance has come in slower with the passage of \nthe front which could lead to showers lingering through the \nmorning Saturday. Weak high pressure is then progged to move in \nreturning dry conditions for the remainder Saturday.\n\nHeavy Rain and Storms Early Next Week: A dynamic synoptic system is \nprogged to lift northeast across the northern Plains and western \nGreat Lakes late Sunday into Monday. Global ensembles show a \nstrong mass response ahead of this system with PWATS increasing \nnear 2\" which is around 200% of normal for this time of year. The \nhigh moisture content and strong dynamical lift does support an \nincreased risk for heavy rainfall with NBM probs showing 30-50% \nchances greater than 2\" of rain across much of the forecast area. \nThere is also a low risk for a few stronger storms to develop \nMonday as strong ~50kt deep layer shear overspreads an unstable \nairmass, however, forecast soundings show a stout inversion near \nthe surface which may keep storms elevated rather than surface \nbased.",
